<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibadan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibadan is a city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. It grew rapidly in the nineteenth century as refugees and warriors settled there after the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>. It became one of the most powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> states of the period and later one of the largest cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibadan.jpg" alt="Ibadan" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ExploreWithTunde · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_the_National_Museum_of_Unity,_Aleshinloye,_Ibadan_2024-06-02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ibadan is the capital of Oyo State, about 120 kilometres inland northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>. Its name is said to come from Eba-Odan, meaning by the edge of the meadow. The site is a forest of several ranges of hills between 160 and 275 metres high, which offered strategic defence, and its position at the fringe of the forest made it a trading point between forest and grassland. The principal inhabitants are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>, with communities from other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. Parts of the city's old protective walls still stand.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ibadan came into existence in 1829, during a period of turmoil in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorubaland">Yorubaland</a> in which older cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-ile">Old Oyo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijaye">Ijaye</a> and Owu disappeared and newer ones such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abeokuta">Abeokuta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-oyo">New Oyo</a> and Ibadan arose. According to local historians Lagelu founded it, at first as a war camp for warriors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a>, Ifẹ and Ijebu. It began as a military state and kept that character until the last decade of the 19th century. Historians have called it a military republic, unlike most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> cities with hereditary kingship; four chiefs governed it, three of whose offices were open to all freeborn males and one reserved for women.</p><p>Warriors were the dominant class and military success offered a path of advancement, which earned it the derision of other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>. Between the 1860s and 1890s it was the centre of an empire over much of northern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorubaland">Yorubaland</a>, nicknamed idi-Ibon, gun base.</p><p>Refugees came in large numbers from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a> after raids by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fula</a> warriors, and in 1840 Ibadan's armies decisively defeated an attempt by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> to expand southwards. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and Anna Hinderer of the Church Missionary Society arrived in 1853 and founded a mission and the city's first Western schools. In 1893 the area became a British protectorate after a treaty signed by Fijabi, the Baale of Ibadan, with the acting Governor of Lagos Colony, George C. Denton, on 15 August. After a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> boom from 1901 to 1913, cocoa became the main product. Ibadan was made headquarters of the Western Provinces in 1939.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>At independence in 1960 Ibadan was the most populous city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and the second most populous in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. Its metropolitan area now has some four million people. The University of Ibadan was founded in 1948 as a college of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and became autonomous in 1962. The University College Hospital was the first teaching hospital in Nigeria. Western Nigeria Television, begun at Ibadan in 1959, was the first television station in Africa. Cocoa House at Dugbe was the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skyscraper">skyscraper</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, and Bower's Tower, built in 1936, stands on Oke Aàre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ibadan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Refugees and warriors from the Oyo heartland founded or enlarged new centres such as <b>Ibadan</b>, Abeokuta and Ijaye, which became the leading Yoruba powers of the nineteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 19th century <b>Ibadan's</b> expansion brought defeat and occupation, and the Ijesha warlord Ogedengbe rose to lead resistance.</blockquote><a href="/state/ijesha-kingdom">Ijesha Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
